```java
public class TextTools {
    /** Returns a String containing the characters of str, in their original order,
     *  with every vowel (a, e, i, o, u, in either upper or lower case) removed. */
    public static String removeVowels(String str)
    { /* to be implemented in part (a) */ }

    /** Returns the number of times sub appears in str. Occurrences may overlap.
     *  Precondition: sub.length() > 0 */
    public static int countSub(String str, String sub)
    { /* to be implemented in part (b) */ }
}
```

**(a)** Write the method `removeVowels`. For example, `removeVowels("Programming")` returns `"Prgrmmng"`, and `removeVowels("AEIOU")` returns the empty string.

**(b)** Write the method `countSub`. The only `String` methods you may use are `length`, `substring`, `indexOf` (either form) and `equals`. For example, `countSub("banana", "ana")` returns 2 (the occurrences start at indexes 1 and 3), `countSub("aaaa", "aa")` returns 3 and `countSub("abc", "x")` returns 0.

sampleResponse:
```java
public static String removeVowels(String str) {
    String result = "";
    for (int i = 0; i < str.length(); i++) {
        String ch = str.substring(i, i + 1);
        if ("aeiouAEIOU".indexOf(ch) < 0) {
            result += ch;
        }
    }
    return result;
}

public static int countSub(String str, String sub) {
    int count = 0;
    int pos = str.indexOf(sub);
    while (pos >= 0) {
        count++;
        pos = str.indexOf(sub, pos + 1);
    }
    return count;
}
```
In (b), searching again from `pos + 1` (not `pos + sub.length()`) is what lets overlapping matches be counted.

