```

**(a)** Write the method `larger`.

**(b)** Write the method `stacked`.

**(c)** Write the method `scaleToArea`. For example, if `r` is a 2 by 3 rectangle, then `scaleToArea(r, 24)` returns a 4 by 6 rectangle.

sampleResponse:
```java
public static Rectangle larger(Rectangle a, Rectangle b) {
    if (b.area() > a.area()) {
        return b;
    }
    return a;
}

public static Rectangle stacked(Rectangle a, Rectangle b) {
    return new Rectangle(Math.max(a.getWidth(), b.getWidth()), a.getHeight() + b.getHeight());
}

public static Rectangle scaleToArea(Rectangle r, double targetArea) {
    return r.scaled(Math.sqrt(targetArea / r.area()));
}
```
Scaling both sides by a factor `k` multiplies the area by $k^2$, so to reach `targetArea` the factor is $k=\sqrt{\text{targetArea}/\text{area}}$.
